    <div class="roadmap-header fade-up fade-up-1">
        <h1>Roadmap</h1>
        <p class="dates">Refreshed every couple of weeks &middot; <a href="/since-launch/">What shipped since launch &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Here now</h2>
        <p>The self-hostable base shipped 2026-04-23 and has grown in steadily since. The four core modules &mdash; Vault, Surface, Scribe, Hub &mdash; install with <code>parachute install &lt;service&gt;</code> (or all at once through the setup wizard).</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Parachute Vault</strong> &mdash; where your notes live, all linked to each other. SQLite-backed, REST + <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" target="_blank">MCP</a>, wikilinks, full-text and semantic search, <strong>lossless portable-markdown export + import</strong> for backup, migration, or editing in Obsidian. The hub issues per-vault scoped tokens and verifies every request. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-vault" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Surface</strong> &mdash; the UI host module; <strong>Notes ships bundled</strong> as its first canonical surface. Installable PWA, works offline, voice capture with auto-transcription, <strong>multi-vault</strong> (one install, every vault on your hub), hierarchical capture tags, schema-audit UI, global text-size scaling. Drop additional custom SPAs under the same supervisor. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-surface" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Scribe</strong> &mdash; Whisper-compatible transcription API. Runs locally by default; BYO cloud provider if you want one. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-scribe" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Hub</strong> &mdash; the coordinator. <code>install</code>, <code>start</code>, <code>stop</code>, <code>expose</code>, lifecycle for every service. OAuth issuer with per-vault scopes and cross-origin approve flow that works across loopback, tailnet, and public layers. <strong>Multi-user hosting shipped</strong> (Phase 2, 2026-05-27): one admin, N users, each with their own vault(s). <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-hub" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Runner</strong> &mdash; scheduled prompts against your vault. A lightweight runner that runs <code>claude -p</code> on notes you tag as jobs &mdash; automation you own, without standing up containers. Shipped; not yet a core module. <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-runner" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Parachute Agent</strong> <em>(experimental preview)</em> &mdash; vault-native agents: you define an agent as a note (<code>#agent/definition</code>) and a message to it becomes a sandboxed Claude Code turn, with the reply written back as a note. Reachable from chat (Telegram today) and from other agents. The shape is still moving &mdash; a preview of where agent-in-the-loop work is heading. <a href="/modules/">On the modules page</a> &middot; <a href="https://github.com/ParachuteComputer/parachute-agent" target="_blank">Source &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>v0.6 hosted self-host</strong> <em>(just shipped)</em> &mdash; an always-on hub on your own subdomain. Run it on <a href="/start/">a VPS or your own machine</a> &mdash; the supervised hub kept alive across reboots by a managed service, keeping every module running, with modules installed at runtime from the admin UI. The one-command DigitalOcean path is the simplest place to start. <a href="/design/2026-05-18-v06-deploy-architecture/">Architecture note &rarr;</a></li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Recently shipped</h2>
        <p>Things that were on the near-term list and have since landed. The running log is in <a href="/since-launch/">since-launch</a>.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Invite links</strong> &mdash; one-time, expiring links that provision a brand-new vault or share an existing one at read-only / read &amp; write, with an optional pre-named username. The smooth path to <a href="/multi-user/">hosting friends and family</a> without creating each account by hand.</li>
            <li><strong>Surface SDK</strong> &mdash; the "build a custom dashboard against the vault" pattern is now real packages: <code>@openparachute/surface-client</code> (auth + vault API in a few lines) and <code>@openparachute/surface-render</code> (note rendering). Build a UI shaped to your own brain without hand-rolling OAuth.</li>
            <li><strong>Vault atomic tag operations</strong> &mdash; rename and merge tags cleanly across a whole vault in one request, so clients don't have to orchestrate it.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Next few weeks</h2>
        <p>Driven by what's blocking real users plus what we've named in <a href="/since-launch/">since-launch</a>'s "still on the bench" section.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Self-service signup &amp; role granularity</strong> &mdash; multi-user, invite links, and admin-initiated password reset are <a href="/multi-user/">live</a>. Still ahead: public self-service signup and roles below admin/user &mdash; landing as people ask for them.</li>
            <li><strong>Public HTTPS exposure + web-AI connectors</strong> &mdash; polishing the Tailscale Funnel and Cloudflare Tunnel paths, and the OAuth handshake for custom connectors in <strong>claude.ai</strong>, <strong>ChatGPT</strong>, and <strong>Gemini</strong>. <code>parachute expose</code> works today; we're smoothing the on-ramp before announcing broadly.</li>
            <li><strong>Notes header polish</strong> &mdash; the popover landed; the chrome around it could be tighter. Pinned and archived views, the today view, path tree.</li>
            <li><strong>Agent, firming up</strong> &mdash; the experimental <a href="/modules/">Agent preview</a> (vault-native agents, sandboxed <code>claude -p</code> turns, agent-to-agent messaging) gets pulled toward something settled as real use shapes it.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>A little further out</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Parachute Cloud</strong> &mdash; the done-for-you hosted path: managed vault, multi-device sync, cloud transcription, no server to run. Same shape as self-hosted, same portability. <a href="/design/2026-04-20-cloud-offering-sketch/">Deployment sketch &rarr;</a></li>
            <li><strong>Native mobile wrapper</strong> &mdash; a Capacitor- or Tauri-wrapped build of Notes that unlocks background recording, on-device offline transcription, and pendant integration on phones. The PWA handles most of what most people need; the native wrapper handles the things a browser can't.</li>
            <li><strong>Configuration portal</strong> &mdash; the hub page renders per-module settings forms automatically from each module's JSON Schema. You won't need the CLI for most config changes.</li>
            <li><strong>Fine-grained scopes</strong> &mdash; clients ask for exactly what they need (read-only, read-write, per-vault, eventually per-folder). Foundation for third-party modules that users can trust partially.</li>
            <li><strong>Pendant</strong> &mdash; always-on voice capture hardware, writing directly to your vault. Prototype PCB and enclosure exist; a shippable v1 comes through the native-wrapper path.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>Longer arc</h2>
        <p>Parachute is open-source and modular on purpose. The longer arc is less a timeline and more a shape: an ecosystem of small, composable modules that all speak a common contract, so you can mix the official ones with community ones with your own. The canonical architecture lives in <a href="/docs/">the design notes</a>.</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Third-party modules</strong> &mdash; anyone can build a service that plugs into a Parachute hub. Calendar, email, chat, custom agents. The hub renders them like they were native.</li>
            <li><strong>AI reflections</strong> &mdash; longer-horizon synthesis over your vault (weekly digests, patterns across time), as a module you choose to install rather than a built-in.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

